exports[`default off-mode surface baseline > locks inspect, grep, find, Pi guidance, and MCP instructions/tool descriptions and schemas 1`] = `
{
  "guidance": {
    "LSP": {
      "guidelines": [
        "LSP { operation, ... } provides strict compiler/language-server semantics: definitions, declarations, references, implementations, hover, document/workspace symbols, type/call hierarchy, diagnostics, and refactor/code-action proposals.",
        "Positions are 0-based in server.positionEncoding.",
        "Rename, formatting, and resolved code-action (resolveCodeAction) results are proposals only; when SmartEdit is loaded they include a proposalId, and applyProposal is the only mutating operation — it applies that staged proposal through SmartEdit's evidence-checked edit path.",
        "Rule of thumb: if the question is 'what does the language server/compiler know about this exact symbol/location?', use LSP. If it is 'what is the shape, architecture, blast radius, graph, or quality of this file/repo?', use inspect.",
      ],
      "snippet": "LSP answers exact compiler/language-server semantic questions — the only path to safe rename/refactor proposals.",
    },
    "find": {
      "guidelines": [
        "find locates files and directories only — never line ranges (those stay in grep). For line-level content search inside files use grep instead.",
      ],
      "snippet": "find locates files and directories by glob, name fragment, or description.",
    },
    "grep": {
      "guidelines": [
        "grep discovers candidates with lexical/BM25/symbol/semantic layers and batch queries; use structural for AST-grep matching and graphFilter for graph-constrained discovery.",
        "Grep returns search-match evidence only, so read the source before editing it.",
      ],
      "snippet": "grep discovers candidates across text, symbols, and concepts — start broad code discovery here.",
    },
    "inspect": {
      "guidelines": [
        "inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture: file mode gives dependencies/dependents, call graph, impact, dead code, routes, and quality signals; directory mode gives the ranked map, clusters, layers, service boundaries, and hotspots.",
        "Inspect does not expose LSP navigation or diagnostics: for compiler-known facts about an exact symbol/location use LSP instead.",
        "inspect { mode: 'script', script } composes a bounded dependent multi-hop investigation when later grep/read/LSP/graph calls depend on earlier results. Do not use script for a lookup that one direct tool call covers.",
      ],
      "snippet": "inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture of a known file or the repository — never raw content reads.",
    },
    "read": {
      "guidelines": [
        "read takes exactly one selector: { path, offset?, limit? } for one known file, { paths: [...] } for several, or { symbol, offset?, limit? } for an already-known qualified symbol. read has no natural-language query mode.",
        "Only complete rendered read blocks provide strong evidence for patch; partial or omitted packed blocks are not authorized.",
        "Large supported source files read without offset/limit may return a compact AST outline instead of the full body, so use offset/limit or symbol for specific slices.",
      ],
      "snippet": "read returns already-known source content with strong workspace evidence — no discovery; use grep or find to locate content first.",
    },
    "skill": {
      "guidelines": [
        "skill: discover and read reusable agent workflow skills.",
      ],
      "snippet": "skill discovers and reads reusable agent workflow skills.",
    },
  },
  "mcpInstructions": "SmartRead Tool Guide
In standalone MCP the Pi-only wrapped \`read\` tool is not exposed: use your host's own file reading in place of SmartRead read.
Tool split: grep discovers candidates, read returns already-known source content, inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture, and LSP answers exact compiler/language-server semantic questions. Only complete rendered read blocks provide strong evidence for patch:
- read: read returns already-known source content with strong workspace evidence — no discovery; use grep or find to locate content first. read takes exactly one selector: { path, offset?, limit? } for one known file, { paths: [...] } for several, or { symbol, offset?, limit? } for an already-known qualified symbol. read has no natural-language query mode. Only complete rendered read blocks provide strong evidence for patch; partial or omitted packed blocks are not authorized. Large supported source files read without offset/limit may return a compact AST outline instead of the full body, so use offset/limit or symbol for specific slices.
- grep: grep discovers candidates across text, symbols, and concepts — start broad code discovery here. grep discovers candidates with lexical/BM25/symbol/semantic layers and batch queries; use structural for AST-grep matching and graphFilter for graph-constrained discovery. Grep returns search-match evidence only, so read the source before editing it.
- find: find locates files and directories by glob, name fragment, or description. find locates files and directories only — never line ranges (those stay in grep). For line-level content search inside files use grep instead.
- inspect: inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture of a known file or the repository — never raw content reads. inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture: file mode gives dependencies/dependents, call graph, impact, dead code, routes, and quality signals; directory mode gives the ranked map, clusters, layers, service boundaries, and hotspots. Inspect does not expose LSP navigation or diagnostics: for compiler-known facts about an exact symbol/location use LSP instead. inspect { mode: 'script', script } composes a bounded dependent multi-hop investigation when later grep/read/LSP/graph calls depend on earlier results. Do not use script for a lookup that one direct tool call covers.
- LSP: LSP answers exact compiler/language-server semantic questions — the only path to safe rename/refactor proposals. LSP { operation, ... } provides strict compiler/language-server semantics: definitions, declarations, references, implementations, hover, document/workspace symbols, type/call hierarchy, diagnostics, and refactor/code-action proposals. Positions are 0-based in server.positionEncoding. Rename, formatting, and resolved code-action (resolveCodeAction) results are proposals only; when SmartEdit is loaded they include a proposalId, and applyProposal is the only mutating operation — it applies that staged proposal through SmartEdit's evidence-checked edit path. Rule of thumb: if the question is 'what does the language server/compiler know about this exact symbol/location?', use LSP. If it is 'what is the shape, architecture, blast radius, graph, or quality of this file/repo?', use inspect.
- skill: skill discovers and reads reusable agent workflow skills. skill: discover and read reusable agent workflow skills.
Prefer narrow params. Large unbounded source reads may return a compact AST outline instead of the full source body, so use offset/limit or symbol for specific slices. After code changes, re-run the reads/inspects/LSP checks that informed decisions.",
  "mcpReadNote": "In standalone MCP the Pi-only wrapped \`read\` tool is not exposed: use your host's own file reading in place of SmartRead read.",
  "piGuide": "Tool split: grep discovers candidates, read returns already-known source content, inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture, and LSP answers exact compiler/language-server semantic questions. Only complete rendered read blocks provide strong evidence for patch:
- read: read returns already-known source content with strong workspace evidence — no discovery; use grep or find to locate content first. read takes exactly one selector: { path, offset?, limit? } for one known file, { paths: [...] } for several, or { symbol, offset?, limit? } for an already-known qualified symbol. read has no natural-language query mode. Only complete rendered read blocks provide strong evidence for patch; partial or omitted packed blocks are not authorized. Large supported source files read without offset/limit may return a compact AST outline instead of the full body, so use offset/limit or symbol for specific slices.
- grep: grep discovers candidates across text, symbols, and concepts — start broad code discovery here. grep discovers candidates with lexical/BM25/symbol/semantic layers and batch queries; use structural for AST-grep matching and graphFilter for graph-constrained discovery. Grep returns search-match evidence only, so read the source before editing it.
- find: find locates files and directories by glob, name fragment, or description. find locates files and directories only — never line ranges (those stay in grep). For line-level content search inside files use grep instead.
- inspect: inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture of a known file or the repository — never raw content reads. inspect analyzes aggregate structure/architecture: file mode gives dependencies/dependents, call graph, impact, dead code, routes, and quality signals; directory mode gives the ranked map, clusters, layers, service boundaries, and hotspots. Inspect does not expose LSP navigation or diagnostics: for compiler-known facts about an exact symbol/location use LSP instead. inspect { mode: 'script', script } composes a bounded dependent multi-hop investigation when later grep/read/LSP/graph calls depend on earlier results. Do not use script for a lookup that one direct tool call covers.
- LSP: LSP answers exact compiler/language-server semantic questions — the only path to safe rename/refactor proposals. LSP { operation, ... } provides strict compiler/language-server semantics: definitions, declarations, references, implementations, hover, document/workspace symbols, type/call hierarchy, diagnostics, and refactor/code-action proposals. Positions are 0-based in server.positionEncoding. Rename, formatting, and resolved code-action (resolveCodeAction) results are proposals only; when SmartEdit is loaded they include a proposalId, and applyProposal is the only mutating operation — it applies that staged proposal through SmartEdit's evidence-checked edit path. Rule of thumb: if the question is 'what does the language server/compiler know about this exact symbol/location?', use LSP. If it is 'what is the shape, architecture, blast radius, graph, or quality of this file/repo?', use inspect.
- skill: skill discovers and reads reusable agent workflow skills. skill: discover and read reusable agent workflow skills.
Prefer narrow params. Large unbounded source reads may return a compact AST outline instead of the full source body, so use offset/limit or symbol for specific slices. After code changes, re-run the reads/inspects/LSP checks that informed decisions.",
  "tools": [
    {
      "description": "Find files and directories by glob pattern, name fragment, or natural-language description. Returns matching paths grouped by directory, relative to the search root. Respects .gitignore. For line-level content search inside files use grep instead.",
      "name": "find",
      "schema": {
        "properties": {
          "limit": {
            "description": "Maximum number of results (default: 100, 20 for natural language; max 500)",
            "type": "number",
          },
          "path": {
            "description": "Directory to search in (default: current directory)",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "pattern": {
            "description": "Glob pattern, file/dir name fragment, or natural-language description, e.g. '*.ts', 'auth', or 'files that configure the embedding endpoint'",
            "type": "string",
          },
        },
        "required": [
          "pattern",
        ],
        "type": "object",
      },
    },
    {
      "description": "Search code for one or more text patterns, symbol names, or concepts. This is the primary broad/textual discovery tool. Provide exactly one of pattern or queries; queries batches up to 10 full searches. Narrow with path, glob, literal, perQueryLimit, contextLines, and maxResults; graphFilter uses "EDGE_TYPE->target"; structural enables ast-grep search. Pattern matching is literal unless compact regex syntax is detected (multi-line patterns and prose with parenthesised asides stay on the smart cascade), literal:true always forces substring matching, and regex:true forces regex matching (pattern must be a valid regex). When auto-detected regex matches nothing on multi-word text, grep falls back to the smart cascade and says so; explicit regex:true never falls back. After discovery, use read for source content at known paths. Use LSP for exact compiler-backed semantics such as definitions, references, types, hierarchy, diagnostics, and refactor safety. Use inspect for aggregate structural or architectural analysis of a known file/directory, and inspect script only for dependent multi-hop chases.",
      "name": "grep",
      "schema": {
        "description": "Primary textual/code discovery search. Provide exactly one of pattern or queries. Narrow with path/glob/literal/regex/perQueryLimit/contextLines; use graphFilter for graph-constrained hits and structural for ast-grep queries. Use LSP instead when exact semantic symbol resolution, references, types, hierarchy, or diagnostics are required.",
        "properties": {
          "contextLines": {
            "default": 2,
            "description": "Lines of context per match (default: 2, max: 20). Applies after the global output cap.",
            "maximum": 20,
            "minimum": 0,
            "type": "number",
          },
          "glob": {
            "description": "File filter, e.g. '*.ts' or 'src/**/*.py'.",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "graphFilter": {
            "description": "Filter results by graph relationship. Format: "EDGE_TYPE->target" e.g. "CALLS->auth.login" or "IMPORTED_BY->src/core".",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "ignoreCase": {
            "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false).",
            "type": "boolean",
          },
          "limit": {
            "default": 20,
            "description": "Deprecated alias for per-query limit (default: 20, max: 100). Use perQueryLimit instead; perQueryLimit wins when both are given.",
            "maximum": 100,
            "minimum": 1,
            "type": "number",
          },
          "literal": {
            "description": "Force exact substring. Disables regex auto-detect, BM25, and semantic (default: false).",
            "type": "boolean",
          },
          "maxResults": {
            "description": "Global max rendered hits across all queries after cross-query dedup (default: 100, max: 200).",
            "maximum": 200,
            "minimum": 1,
            "type": "number",
          },
          "path": {
            "description": "Directory or file to search in (default: cwd).",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "pattern": {
            "description": "Literal substring by default. Compact regex syntax (|, ^, $, [class], {n}, \\d/\\w/\\s/\\b, \\. or compact foo.*bar / (group) without inner spaces) auto-routes to regex. Multi-line patterns, prose with parenthesised asides, and isolated .* / .+ in multi-word text stay on the smart cascade. Set regex:true to force regex (must be valid, never falls back) or literal:true to force substring. If auto-detected regex matches nothing on multi-word text, grep falls back to the smart cascade and says so. Provide pattern or queries, not both.",
            "minLength": 1,
            "type": "string",
          },
          "perQueryLimit": {
            "description": "Max results per query (default: 20, max: 50). Wins over deprecated limit when both are given.",
            "maximum": 50,
            "minimum": 1,
            "type": "number",
          },
          "queries": {
            "description": "Multiple searches to run in one call. Top-level options are shared defaults; per-query options override them.",
            "items": {
              "properties": {
                "contextLines": {
                  "default": 2,
                  "description": "Lines of context per match (default: 2, max: 20). Applies after the global output cap.",
                  "maximum": 20,
                  "minimum": 0,
                  "type": "number",
                },
                "glob": {
                  "description": "File filter, e.g. '*.ts' or 'src/**/*.py'.",
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "graphFilter": {
                  "description": "Filter results by graph relationship. Format: "EDGE_TYPE->target" e.g. "CALLS->auth.login" or "IMPORTED_BY->src/core".",
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "ignoreCase": {
                  "description": "Case-insensitive search (default: false).",
                  "type": "boolean",
                },
                "limit": {
                  "default": 20,
                  "description": "Deprecated alias for per-query limit (default: 20, max: 100). Use perQueryLimit instead; perQueryLimit wins when both are given.",
                  "maximum": 100,
                  "minimum": 1,
                  "type": "number",
                },
                "literal": {
                  "description": "Force exact substring. Disables regex auto-detect, BM25, and semantic (default: false).",
                  "type": "boolean",
                },
                "path": {
                  "description": "Directory or file to search in (default: cwd).",
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "pattern": {
                  "description": "Literal substring by default. Compact regex syntax (|, ^, $, [class], {n}, \\d/\\w/\\s/\\b, \\. or compact foo.*bar / (group) without inner spaces) auto-routes to regex. Multi-line patterns, prose with parenthesised asides, and isolated .* / .+ in multi-word text stay on the smart cascade. Set regex:true to force regex (must be valid, never falls back) or literal:true to force substring. If auto-detected regex matches nothing on multi-word text, grep falls back to the smart cascade and says so.",
                  "minLength": 1,
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "perQueryLimit": {
                  "description": "Max results per query (default: 20, max: 50). Wins over deprecated limit when both are given.",
                  "maximum": 50,
                  "minimum": 1,
                  "type": "number",
                },
                "regex": {
                  "description": "Force regex matching. Pattern must be a valid regex; conflicts with literal:true.",
                  "type": "boolean",
                },
                "skip": {
                  "description": "Matches to skip (pagination) for structural search — routes into structural.skip.",
                  "minimum": 0,
                  "type": "number",
                },
                "structural": {
                  "description": "Structural search options (ast-grep).",
                  "properties": {
                    "groupByFile": {
                      "description": "Group matches by file.",
                      "type": "boolean",
                    },
                    "language": {
                      "description": "Structural language override (e.g. 'typescript', 'python').",
                      "type": "string",
                    },
                    "skip": {
                      "description": "Matches to skip (pagination).",
                      "minimum": 0,
                      "type": "number",
                    },
                  },
                  "type": "object",
                },
              },
              "required": [
                "pattern",
              ],
              "type": "object",
            },
            "maxItems": 10,
            "minItems": 1,
            "type": "array",
          },
          "regex": {
            "description": "Force regex matching. Pattern must be a valid regex; conflicts with literal:true.",
            "type": "boolean",
          },
          "skip": {
            "description": "Matches to skip (pagination) for structural search — routes into structural.skip.",
            "minimum": 0,
            "type": "number",
          },
          "structural": {
            "description": "Structural search options (ast-grep).",
            "properties": {
              "groupByFile": {
                "description": "Group matches by file.",
                "type": "boolean",
              },
              "language": {
                "description": "Structural language override (e.g. 'typescript', 'python').",
                "type": "string",
              },
              "skip": {
                "description": "Matches to skip (pagination).",
                "minimum": 0,
                "type": "number",
              },
            },
            "type": "object",
          },
        },
        "type": "object",
      },
    },
    {
      "description": "Inspect aggregate code structure and repository architecture for already-known targets. Use { mode: "file", path, analysis? } for structural facts such as dependencies/dependents, call graph, impact, dead code, routes, diff mapping, and quality signals. Use { mode: "directory", path, analysis? } for repository maps, graph summaries, clusters, layers, service boundaries, hotspots, routes, and other architectural views. Use { mode: "script", script, path? } only for bounded dependent multi-hop investigations. Do not use inspect for exact compiler-backed semantic lookup or navigation. Use LSP for definitions, references, implementations, hover, document/workspace symbols, type/call hierarchy, diagnostics, completion/signature/inlay information, and refactor proposals. Use read when you simply need the contents of a known file.",
      "name": "inspect",
      "schema": {
        "additionalProperties": false,
        "description": "Inspect known code structure and repository architecture. Modes: file (aggregate structural facts + quality signals), directory (repo map + architecture), script (bounded dependent multi-hop composition). Use LSP instead for exact compiler/language-server semantics such as definitions, references, hover, symbols, type/call hierarchy, diagnostics, completion, and refactor proposals.",
        "properties": {
          "analysis": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "additionalProperties": false,
                "properties": {
                  "callDepth": {
                    "description": "BFS call graph traversal depth (1-5, default 1).",
                    "maximum": 5,
                    "minimum": 1,
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                  "callDirection": {
                    "anyOf": [
                      {
                        "const": "callers",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "callees",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "both",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                    ],
                    "description": "Call graph traversal direction. Requires callDepth.",
                  },
                  "compact": {
                    "description": "Compact output (default false for file).",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "deadCode": {
                    "description": "Return zero-caller functions in scope.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "diff": {
                    "anyOf": [
                      {
                        "const": "unstaged",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "staged",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "HEAD",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                    ],
                    "description": "Map git diff to affected symbols with risk classification.",
                  },
                  "graphSchema": {
                    "description": "Return graph structure summary (node/edge counts, sample names).",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "hotspots": {
                    "description": "Top-N functions by fan-in.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "impact": {
                    "description": "Blast radius: files/symbols reachable from target.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "routes": {
                    "description": "Extract HTTP route → handler mappings.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "signals": {
                    "description": "Signals to compute (default: all).",
                    "items": {
                      "anyOf": [
                        {
                          "const": "complexity",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                        {
                          "const": "public-api",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                        {
                          "const": "reuse",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                        {
                          "const": "recency",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                        {
                          "const": "tests",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                        {
                          "const": "deprecation",
                          "type": "string",
                        },
                      ],
                    },
                    "type": "array",
                  },
                },
                "type": "object",
              },
              {
                "additionalProperties": false,
                "properties": {
                  "boundaries": {
                    "description": "Detect service boundaries from monorepo config.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "clusters": {
                    "description": "Run community detection on import graph.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "compact": {
                    "description": "Compact output (default true for directory).",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "deadCode": {
                    "description": "Return zero-caller functions in scope.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "diff": {
                    "anyOf": [
                      {
                        "const": "unstaged",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "staged",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                      {
                        "const": "HEAD",
                        "type": "string",
                      },
                    ],
                    "description": "Map git diff to affected symbols with risk classification.",
                  },
                  "focus": {
                    "description": "Files/symbols to boost in directory mode.",
                    "items": {
                      "type": "string",
                    },
                    "type": "array",
                  },
                  "graphSchema": {
                    "description": "Return graph structure summary (node/edge counts, sample names).",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "hotspots": {
                    "description": "Top-N functions by fan-in.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "layers": {
                    "description": "Derive architectural layers.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                  "mapTokens": {
                    "description": "Token budget for directory mode (256-32768, default 4096).",
                    "maximum": 32768,
                    "minimum": 256,
                    "type": "number",
                  },
                  "routes": {
                    "description": "Extract HTTP route → handler mappings.",
                    "type": "boolean",
                  },
                },
                "type": "object",
              },
            ],
            "description": "Aggregate structural analysis only: file signals/call graph/impact/dead-code/routes, or directory repo map/clusters/layers/boundaries. Do not use for definitions, references, hover, language-server symbols, or diagnostics; use LSP for those. Fields belonging to the other inspect mode are rejected at runtime.",
          },
          "mode": {
            "anyOf": [
              {
                "const": "file",
                "type": "string",
              },
              {
                "const": "directory",
                "type": "string",
              },
              {
                "const": "script",
                "type": "string",
              },
            ],
            "description": "Structural/contextual inspect mode. Use file for one artifact, directory for repository architecture, or script for a dependent multi-hop read-only investigation. For compiler-backed semantic lookup/navigation use LSP instead.",
          },
          "path": {
            "description": "Known file or directory target. Required for file/directory; optional cwd anchor for script.",
            "type": "string",
          },
          "script": {
            "description": "WHEN:
- running a multi-hop investigation where each call's arguments depend on the previous call's result (grep a symbol, then LSP references on the hit, then graph impact on those files) that would otherwise cost 3+ sequential round trips

WHEN NOT:
- answering a single lookup that one grep, read, or inspect call already covers
- reading full file prose directly (use plain read; script mode returns a synthesized JSON result, not raw file text)

RETURNS: the script's JSON return value plus a bounded per-call audit log naming each op, its args, the path or resource touched, and its status.

EXAMPLE: { mode: "script", script: "const g = await grep(\\"handleAuth\\", { literal: true }); const r = await read(\\"src/auth.ts\\"); return { hits: g.totalHits, lines: r.totalLines };" }",
            "minLength": 1,
            "type": "string",
          },
        },
        "required": [
          "mode",
        ],
        "type": "object",
      },
    },
  ],
}
